ourse we've we've had the Danes and  the Vikings we've had the Normans we've  had people coming in with its Huguenots  or people fleeing from persecution  across the world the Windrush  interesting about the wind rush because  when they came 70 years ago they saw  Britain as the motherland so the borders  that they were seeing were borders based  on geography they were based on what  they'd been ain't what inculcation had  taken place in terms of a a combined  history I don't think people would see  that now they didn't get a great great  welcome from the motherland as we all  know I've tried and perhaps we come back  to this to say that developing common  values for citizenship and recognizing  that wherever anyone came from is the  real challenge for the future thank you  very much David okay last but not least  Eric your thought to this question of  borders simply being an accident of  history right so the question is our  borders accident of history and I think  the truth is in part they are I mean as  Charles Tilly the political historian  once said national borders are the  high-water point of dynastic  expansionism and we know that  post-colonial of nation-states a lot of  their borders are straight lines which  were drawn by the colonial power so yes  borders are in some degree an accident  of history but at the same time we also  know that collective will has also  shaped the location of national borders  if we go back to ancient Israel and if  you look in the Old Testament talking  about the borders of Israel from Dan to  Beersheba now there of course there many  concepts of the Promised Land in the  Bible that's only one of them I don't  pretend it's the only one but this is to  say that groups were already talking  about some kind of territorial reference  points even back in ancient times and  certainly as we come through history  there are many instances if you think of  the poles more recently South Sudan or  or East Timor where it's not just great  p